export const DE_C2_S03_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-suessstoff": [
    `I am ninety-five now, and my great-granddaughter, who is studying history, has asked me to write this down before, as she puts it, "it gets lost". As if you could lose a story like a glove. But all right. It was in the summer of 1947, on the Reichenau, and the world for us consisted of two shores: ours, where there was nothing, and the one opposite, where there was everything. Between them lay the Untersee, at its narrowest point barely two kilometres wide, and in those years more goods crossed that lake than the statistics ever found out about.`,
    `Sugar was rationed, and what you got on your coupons wouldn't have filled a sparrow. In Switzerland, on the other hand, there was saccharin, little white tablets three hundred times sweeter than sugar, so that a tin the size of a cigarette packet sweetened a whole household for a whole winter. On our side such a tin was a small fortune. My uncle Fidel, a fisherman by trade and a smuggler by conviction, rowed across to Ermatingen twice a week at night. In June he broke his arm mending nets. And because his sons were prisoners of war and my mother couldn't swim, it was my turn.`,
    `I was sixteen and had been rowing since I could walk. Over there Ruedi was waiting, the son of the fisherman my uncle did business with, a tall, silent lad who handed me the tins in a potato sack into the boat and each time said: "Pass uf, gell" ("Take care, eh"). He never said more. Back then I imagined that those three words contained an entire declaration of love, and I rowed back with the feeling that I was carrying not saccharin but something far more precious across the border.`,
    `On the fourth night he was standing on the shore when I landed: the customs officer. The French had occupied our shore, and at the border stood men in their uniform, many of them Alsatians, because they spoke our language, or at least one similar enough. This one was called Joseph Wendling, I found out later; he was in his mid-twenties and had a lantern in one hand and nothing at all in the other, which seemed more suspicious to me than any rifle. He shone the light into the potato sack and asked, in an Alemannic dialect that sounded like ours, only with foreign edges, what that was.`,
    `Medicine, I said. For my grandmother. She had diabetes. He took out a tin, unscrewed it, put a tablet on his tongue and pulled a face. "For a grandmother," he said, "that's an awful lot of grandmother." Then he put the tin in his coat pocket, wished me good night and left. He left me the rest. I stood there, wet to the knees, understanding nothing, least of all myself, because I wasn't as grateful to him as I should have been, but offended, because he hadn't taken me seriously.`,
    `It became a ritual. All summer long he stood, not every night but often enough, at whatever spot on the shore I landed, although I changed spots the way other girls changed hair ribbons. Every time he shone his light into the sack, every time he asked after my grandmother, whose health deteriorated dramatically over the course of our conversations, and every time he took exactly one tin. "Duty," he said as he did so, and only years later did I understand that he meant the word literally. A duty was a levy, not a prohibition. He was collecting, not confiscating.`,
    `Nothing came of Ruedi. He went on saying "Pass uf, gell", and at some point in August I understood that with him it was not a declaration of love but a weather forecast. With Joseph, on the other hand, I was soon talking for longer than a check required: about the lake, about the war, from which he had come back with a stiff knee and without his brother, about the question of which side of a lake a person really belongs to when the uniforms keep changing colour. The currency reform was still a year away, but in the autumn my uncle could row again, and my career as a smuggler was over.`,
    `We were married in 1951, in Mittelzell, and after the ceremony Joseph put a wicker basket on the table in front of the assembled relatives. In it were thirty-seven tins of saccharin, all unopened but one. He had never handed them in. "Refund of duty," he said, and my uncle Fidel laughed so hard that he choked on the wedding wine. We never used the tins up. One still stands in my kitchen today. Ruedi, by the way, later opened a cake shop in Stein am Rhein which, to the very end, was proud of being the only one on the whole lake never to have used a single gram of sweetener. You can guess why.`,
    `Love, they say, is sweet. Mine had duty paid on it first.`,
  ],
  "de-bericht-ueber-den-kometen": [
    `To the Ducal High Consistory at Weimar. Kranichfeld, 14 October 1811. Most reverend and highly esteemed gentlemen! On the second of this month a High Consistory instructed all the pastors of the district to report conscientiously on the effect of the great comet on the souls entrusted to them. I most obediently comply with this order, although I fear that my report will not turn out in every respect as a High Consistory may wish.`,
    `Since the end of August the heavenly body has stood every evening above the woods to the north-west, and its tail has by now grown so long that the children claim you can no longer encompass it with outstretched arms. The effect on the parish was at first the one to be expected. The linen weaver Rößner sold his cow so as not, as he says, to face the end of the world in debt; the widow Hartung baked her entire store of flour and shared the cake among her neighbours, which set off a charitableness in the village such as I have never achieved with any sermon. A travelling preacher of repentance from the Vogtland spoke in the market square for three evenings and announced the end for St Martin's Day. He found more listeners than I do at Easter.`,
    `I, by contrast, preached on the Sunday after Michaelmas on the nineteenth Psalm: "The heavens declare the glory of God." I explained that, according to everything the learned gentlemen in Bremen and Göttingen write on the matter, a comet is a natural body that follows its orbit as the moon follows its own, and that one should consequently admire it, but not fear it. The congregation listened to me attentively and afterwards went off as one to the preacher of repentance.`,
    `I must furthermore most obediently report what a High Consistory has presumably already learned by other means. The schoolmaster Eckardt owns a telescope which he inherited from an uncle in Jena, and in September he asked me to view the comet with him from the church tower, where the view over the roofs is unobstructed. I agreed, in the conviction that a pastor of souls ought not to know the works of the Creator less well than a schoolmaster. We subsequently climbed the tower on eleven evenings. The bell-ringer saw the light of our lantern, and since then people in the village say the pastor is giving signals to the comet. The widow Hartung asked me whether I couldn't tell it to please wait until after the potato harvest. I promised her I would. I ask that this lie be pardoned; it has given the woman more peace than all of astronomy.`,
    `What I saw through the telescope I can scarcely describe, and I am not sure whether it belongs in an official report. The nucleus is small and sharp like a pinprick, but the tail is not, as one imagines, a fire, but a veil through which one can still make out the stars behind it. The schoolmaster said that the light of these stars had been travelling long before there was any Kranichfeld. At that, God forgive me, for the first time in my ministry I did not know what to answer. We stood a long while in silence among the bells, two grown men, and froze.`,
    `And now I come to the point on account of which I fear that a High Consistory will read this report with disapproval. I have a son, Friedrich, who seven years ago gave up theology against my will to study law in Jena, and who has not set foot in my house since. For seven years I did not write to him, since I was of the opinion that it was for him to take the first step. In the night after the eleventh visit to the tower I sat down and wrote to him. Not because I considered the end of the world at Martinmas likely, but because it dawned on me up there that I had until then treated the time left to me as if it were under my administration.`,
    `He came last Saturday, on foot, with a bottle of this year's wine, which the vintners on the Saale, so he says, are already calling the comet wine. We emptied it together and afterwards, with the schoolmaster, climbed the tower. The bell-ringer counted three lanterns and ran straight to the widow Hartung.`,
    `Whether all this is to be recorded as an effect of the comet on the souls entrusted to me, I leave to the judgement of a High Consistory. It has at any rate not left my own untouched. In due reverence I remain a High Consistory's most obedient servant, Johann Gottfried Ackermann, Pastor.`,
  ],
  "de-das-kraut-von-echterdingen": [
    `In our family there is a picture frame that has hung in the hall for almost a hundred years and that visitors regularly take for a curiosity, which it is. In it, back to back under one glass, are two pieces of paper: a bill for fourteen marks thirty and a letter on fine handmade paper with a count's letterhead. My great-grandfather, Gottlob Haug, a cabbage farmer on the Filder plain, kept both of them, and, as people of his generation used to say, he knew why.`,
    `On 5 August 1908, a sultry Wednesday, Count Zeppelin's airship, forced to land by engine failure on its great twenty-four-hour flight, came down near Echterdingen, on a field not far from the Haug cabbage fields. What happened next is in every school book: within hours tens of thousands came streaming in, on foot, on horseback, by bicycle, from Stuttgart and half the state, to see the silver wonder up close. In the afternoon a squall tore the ship loose; it caught fire and burned down to its frame in a few minutes before the eyes of the crowd.`,
    `What isn't in any school book: that the tens of thousands, before they wept, stood, and for the most part they stood in Gottlob Haug's pointed cabbages. When the smoke had cleared, nothing was left of his two finest acres but a green mush in which lay hatpins, sandwich paper and, as he later stated, a single lady's shoe. Gottlob walked along the rows, counted, calculated, and that same evening sat down at the kitchen table to write a proper bill to His Excellency Count von Zeppelin, Friedrichshafen. After deducting the heads that could still be saved, it came to fourteen marks thirty.`,
    `You have to picture the mood of those days to understand what that meant. The empire was in a frenzy. While the wreckage was still smoking, a collection began such as the country had never seen. Schoolchildren gave their money boxes, maids their weekly wages, veterans' associations their funds; in the end more than six million marks were raised, and anyone who didn't give was considered, to put it mildly, an unpatriotic scoundrel. And in Echterdingen of all places, at the scene of the disaster, sat a man who didn't give but took. Or at any rate wanted to take.`,
    `The village reacted the way villages react. At the baker's, conversation stopped when Gottlob came in. The following Sunday the pastor preached, with a look that left no doubt about whom he meant, on the widow's mite. And some joker painted an airship on Gottlob's barn door in the night, with a head of cabbage hanging from it instead of a gondola. Gottlob left it there. He had, he said, no paint to give away.`,
    `Three weeks later the reply came. The count, or rather a secretary in his name, thanked him with exquisite courtesy for the precise statement, sincerely regretted the damage to the crops and enclosed, as the letter put it, "the amount requested in cash", fourteen marks thirty, to the pfennig. Gottlob read the letter twice, nodded as if he had expected nothing else, put the money in the chest of drawers and never said another word about it. The village considered the matter closed, and Gottlob Haug the only Swabian who had made money from the loss of the airship.`,
    `The truth only came out in 1931, after his death, when my grandfather was sorting out his estate. In the chest of drawers, carefully folded, lay a receipt from the Zeppelin Fund of the German People, made out in September 1908 to an "anonymous donor from Echterdingen", for three hundred marks. It was, as his savings books proved, almost everything Gottlob owned at the time. The receipt had been stuck onto the back of his copy of the bill, and underneath, in his steep, awkward handwriting, was a single sentence.`,
    `My grandfather had both, bill and letter, framed together, so that at the front you read the count and at the back, when you turn the frame round, the farmer. The sentence reads: "You pay what you owe, and you give what you want. That's two different things." In my working life as a tax adviser I have read many clever books on ethics and economics. None of them has put it more precisely.`,
  ],
  "de-nicht-lohnend": [
    `Berlin, 12 March 1903. Dear Widow Gstrein, Allow me to introduce myself as the editor of "Lehmbruck's Travel Handbooks", whose volume "Tyrol and Vorarlberg" is to appear this summer in an eleventh, completely revised edition. A friend of mine, a professor of geology, praises your valley to me as the most beautiful and unspoilt in the Ötztal Alps, and your inn "Zur Gams" as the only place where it can be properly enjoyed. I therefore intend to distinguish Unterwinkl with a star, and very politely ask you for details of the number of beds, the prices and the condition of the path up from the main valley. Your most obedient servant, Dr Konrad Lehmbruck.`,
    `Unterwinkl, Post Sölden, 2 April 1903. Most honoured Doctor, Your letter arrived three weeks late, because in winter the postman only comes up when he has to, and he seldom has to. That may suffice as information about the condition of the path. I have four beds, one of them for the parish priest when he comes for confirmation. I am the cook myself, and the waitress too. The milk tastes of cow, the water of stone and the wine of vinegar, because it is carried up from Bozen and gets into a bad mood on the way. I set no store by a star. The stars over Unterwinkl can be seen without your book. With all respect, Anna Gstrein, widow.`,
    `Berlin, 20 April 1903. Esteemed Madam, Your letter, I confess, has only strengthened my intention. What you describe as shortcomings is exactly what today's traveller is looking for: the genuine, the original, milk that tastes of cow. Progress, madam, cannot be stopped, and it is better that it comes with a conscientious guidebook in its hand than without one. So that my judgement rests on my own observation, I intend to visit you in person in July, under an assumed name, as has always been the custom of my house. Yours, Lehmbruck.`,
    `Unterwinkl, 3 May 1903. Doctor, You are welcome to come under an assumed name. I shall recognise you all the same, because one stranger a year comes to me, and he is usually a geologist, and he knocks on stones, not on doors. But if you come, the following conditions apply. First: no complaining, neither about the bed nor about the weather. Second: in July it is haymaking, and whoever eats at my table rakes as well. Third: on Sunday there is Mass, and the fact that you are a Prussian is no excuse but one reason more. Respectfully, A. G.`,
    `Berlin, 30 September 1903. Dear Frau Gstrein, I have been back in the city for three weeks and still have blisters on my hands, which my doctor takes for a skin disease and I for the finest memory of my life. I have raked hay, not well, but undaunted. I have drunk milk that tasted of cow, and wine that was in a bad mood. On Sunday I sat in the very back pew and understood the priest, although he preached in Tyrolean, or perhaps precisely because of that. And I have, madam, made a decision that will plunge my publisher into despair.`,
    `The entry on Unterwinkl in the eleventh edition will read as follows: "Unterwinkl, 1 hr from the valley floor, steep, poorly maintained path. Inn simple, 4 beds. Food modest. Not worth the trip." I may assure you that no reader of my house has ever visited a place honoured with those last words. Should my bed still be free next July, I would ask you to reserve it for me. Yours, devoted in every respect, Konrad Lehmbruck.`,
    `Unterwinkl, 8 October 1903. Doctor, The bed is free. So is the rake. A. G.`,
    `Note by the editor of these letters: the remark "not worth the trip" appears under the entry Unterwinkl in every edition of "Lehmbruck's Travel Handbooks" up to the last one in 1938. Their author, as the guest book of the "Gams" shows, spent twenty-nine summers in the valley. The last entry in his handwriting reads: "Again not worth it. Thank God."`,
  ],
  "de-die-brieftaube": [
    `Karl-Heinz Pawlowski, known to everyone simply as Kalle, had worked underground at the Ewald colliery in Herten for thirty-three years and, since his retirement, had lived only above ground, as the neighbours put it, or more precisely on the roof. Up there stood his pigeon loft, a wooden structure he looked after with greater care than the flat below it, and in it lived forty-eight racing pigeons, one of which, a grey-blue hen with a white patch on her neck, was in the unanimous opinion of the club "Heimatliebe Herten-Süd" the fastest in the whole Ruhr. Her name was Erika.`,
    `That the hen was called Erika gave rise to gossip on the estate, because Erika had also been the name of Kalle's wife. It still was, only no longer Pawlowski. She had moved out in 1999, after twenty-seven years of marriage, with a sentence that half the street had overheard through the open window: "Kalle, you've got a heart like a pigeon loft. There's room in it for lots, but I'm not one of them." Kalle claimed he had never understood that sentence. Frau Kaczmarek next door claimed he had understood it perfectly well and hadn't said another word about Erika since, except on Sundays at seven, when he called down from the roof, "Erika, come in!", and half the street jumped.`,
    `In June the big prize race from Regensburg was due, a good four hundred and fifty kilometres as the crow flies. As every year, Kalle put his money on Erika. The pigeons were basketed at the club's pub on the Friday and released in Bavaria early on Sunday, and from midday Kalle sat on the roof with the timing clock on his knees and a thermos of coffee beside him. At half past three the first ones arrived. By six they were all back except one, sixteen out of seventeen. He sat there into the night, then all of Monday. "She'll come," he said to Frau Kaczmarek, who handed meatballs up to him on the roof. "She's never not come." On Tuesday he no longer ate the meatballs.`,
    `On Wednesday evening the phone rang. "Kalle," said a voice he hadn't heard for twenty years and yet recognised after one syllable. "I've got one of your pigeons here. Manni from the club gave me the ring number. You can come and get her. Bottrop, Gartenstraße, behind the house." Then she hung up before he could say anything, which was probably just as well.`,
    `Behind the little house in Bottrop stood a pigeon loft. Not big, but clean, painted green, and on the landing board sat Erika the pigeon, among a dozen strange pigeons, preening herself as if nothing had happened. Erika the woman stood next to it with her arms folded. "She didn't get lost, Kalle," she said. "She came home." Racing pigeons, as every child in the Ruhr knew, don't return to their owner but to the loft where they grew up. Kalle had bought Erika as a youngster, through Manni, from a breeder "who doesn't want to be named, but top-notch". Like almost all his best pigeons.`,
    `"Since 2001," said Erika. "After the divorce I got myself three pigeons, out of anger, to understand what you see in the creatures. And then I understood." She shrugged. "And since you liked pigeons better than me anyway, you should at least have mine." She had always given away the other youngsters early, before they flew their first rounds here, so that they would get used to his loft. Only with the grey-blue one had she waited. For years the bird had flown to him all the same, but on a long, hard race one like that sometimes finds its way back to its first loft. "She was supposed to know where I live," she said, and it didn't sound as if she was talking only about the pigeon.`,
    `Kalle said nothing for a long time, which was nothing unusual for him. Then he sat down on the bench in front of the loft, which had clearly been built for two people, and watched the pigeons. Erika went into the house and came back with two cups of coffee. "So what about the pigeon?" he asked at last. "She's staying here," said Erika. "She's home, after all. But you can visit her."`,
    `Since then Kalle has driven to Bottrop every Sunday, to see to the pigeon, as he explains to Frau Kaczmarek. Frau Kaczmarek, in return, tells anyone who will listen that there is now a strange silence on the estate on Sundays at seven. In Bottrop, on the other hand, according to her sister-in-law, who lives two houses along there, a man calls out loudly across the gardens on Sunday mornings, "Erika, come in!" And a woman's voice answers: "I'm coming, I'm coming."`,
  ],
};

